---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
const fig = (n: string) => `${base}/figures/${n}`
const vid = (n: string) => `${base}/videos/${n}`

const authors = [
	{ name: 'Minkyu Kang', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/' },
	{ name: 'JinHo Kim' },
	{ name: 'Eunseop Shin' },
	{ name: 'Dayeon Hwang' },
]

const links = [
	{ label: 'Project Weasel', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/' },
	{ label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/MK040412/Weasel_toy_experiment' },
	{ label: 'Slides', href: 'https://github.com/MK040412/Mean-Collapse-VLA/blob/main/README.md' },
]

const body = String.raw`
<div class="tldr-box">
	<h2>TL;DR</h2>
	<p>
		On <span class="text-tt">OGBench Ant-Maze</span> we train a
		<b>VLM-based VLA</b> in the <a href="https://openvla-oft.github.io/">OpenVLA-OFT</a>
		style on top of a <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21631">Qwen3-VL 2B</a>
		backbone. The instruction is a <b>coordinate VQA</b>: given an image plus
		<span class="text-tt">Go to (x, y)</span>, the model outputs the
		<b>intermediate trajectory waypoint coordinates</b>
		$(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \ldots$
	</p>
	<p>
		<b>Both views mean-collapse</b> when the policy acts on the latest observation
		only — and the <b>top-view (MDP) collapse is more severe</b>: the agent walks
		in place, twitches, falls over, gets frozen. The 3rd-person POMDP rollouts
		actually move more and occasionally find the pink goal opportunistically.
	</p>
	<p>
		A small <b>memory-based steering module</b> (GLA Linear-SSM + Perceiver,
		injected into the <b>frozen</b> VLA) <b>solves mean collapse</b> and raises
		the success rate.
	</p>
	<p>
		The same Markovian-on-$o_t$ pathology shows up on a real GUI agent
		(<a href="https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero">GELab-Zero-4B-preview</a>)
		running a Duolingo &ldquo;tap the matching pairs&rdquo; quiz.
	</p>
</div>

<hr />
<h1>1. MDP vs. POMDP — and Why Mean Collapse Happens</h1>

<p>
	A <b>Markov Decision Process</b> $\mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, P, R, \gamma)$
	gives the agent the underlying state $s_t$ at every step, so the optimal policy
	is Markovian on the state:
</p>

<p>$$\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{MDP}}(a_t \mid s_t).$$</p>

<p>
	A <b>Partially Observable MDP</b>
	$\mathcal{P} = (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}, P, R, \Omega, \gamma)$
	hides the state behind an observation kernel
	$o_t \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid s_t)$. The optimal policy now conditions on the full
	history (or on a belief $b_t$):
</p>

<p>
	$$
	\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{POMDP}}(a_t \mid h_t),\qquad
	h_t = (o_0, a_0, o_1, a_1, \ldots, o_t).
	$$
</p>

<div class="block-statement">
	A Markovian policy on $o_t$ is suboptimal whenever $o_t \not\Rightarrow s_t$.
</div>

<h2>1.1 Why Mean Collapse Happens</h2>

<p>
	A <b>VLM-based VLA trained to act on the latest observation only</b> learns a
	Markovian policy $\pi(a_t \mid o_t)$. On a POMDP input, several distinct
	underlying states can map to similar observations:
	$s^{(1)} \neq s^{(2)} \neq \cdots$ but
	$o^{(1)} \approx o^{(2)} \approx o$ (visual aliasing in 3rd-person Ant-Maze).
	Since the training data is collected from optimal trajectories on the underlying
	$s^{(i)}$, the loss is minimized when the policy produces the
	<b>posterior-weighted average</b> of the true optimal actions:
</p>

<p>
	$$
	\pi^{\star}(a \mid o)
	\;=\; \mathbb{E}_{s \sim p(s \mid o)}\!\left[\pi^{\star}(a \mid s)\right]
	\;=\; \sum_{i} p\!\left(s^{(i)} \mid o\right)\, \pi^{\star}\!\left(a \mid s^{(i)}\right).
	$$
</p>

<p>
	When the optimal $\pi^{\star}(a \mid s^{(i)})$ for different $s^{(i)}$ point in
	<b>opposite directions</b> — &ldquo;turn left&rdquo; vs. &ldquo;turn right&rdquo;,
	&ldquo;step forward&rdquo; vs. &ldquo;step back&rdquo; — their average is a
	<b>degenerate motion</b>: walks in place, sideways drift, falling over, or
	loops between two actions. That is <b>mean collapse</b>, and on Ant-Maze it
	shows up <b>even on the privileged top-view (MDP) input</b> (see Section 5).
</p>

<hr />
<h1>2. Most VLA Benchmarks Hide the POMDP</h1>

<p>
	Most VLA benchmarks are effectively MDPs: the current scene already encodes the
	task-relevant state, so a policy reading only the latest observation looks fine
	(<span class="text-tt">LIBERO</span>, <span class="text-tt">SIMPLER</span>).
	GUI agents (<span class="text-tt">UI-TARS</span>-style) decide each action from a
	<b>summary plus the current screenshot only</b>. Both setups assume
	$\pi(a_t \mid o_t)$ suffices.
</p>
<p>
	But real long-horizon tasks (Minecraft, GUI agents, manipulation under
	occlusion) need <b>history</b> $h_t$, not just $o_t$. Crafting a pickaxe needs
	the fact &ldquo;I already chopped a tree&rdquo; — and that fact is not in the
	current screen.
</p>

<hr />
<h1>3. A Controllable POMDP in OGBench Ant-Maze</h1>

<p>
	We use the public <a href="https://github.com/seohongpark/ogbench">OGBench</a>
	Ant-Maze tasks and re-render them in MuJoCo with two cameras:
</p>

<ul>
	<li><b>Top-view (MDP).</b> $o_t = s_t$: full position, goal, and maze layout.</li>
	<li><b>3rd-person view (POMDP).</b> $o_t \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid s_t)$: only what is in front of the ant. Visually similar 3rd-person frames can correspond to <b>different</b> next steps — the source of mean collapse.</li>
</ul>

<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('two_views.png')}" alt="Same world, two views — the source of mean collapse" />
	<span class="caption">
		Same world, two views. <b>Left:</b> top-view (MDP). <b>Right:</b> 3rd-person
		(POMDP). Visually similar 3rd-person frames can correspond to different
		underlying states.
	</span>
</div>

<hr />
<h1>4. Setup</h1>

<p>
	Concrete numbers, taken from the
	<a href="https://github.com/MK040412/Weasel_toy_experiment">Weasel Toy Experiment</a>
	repository.
</p>

<h2>4.1 Data — AntMaze paired dataset</h2>
<ul>
	<li>Source: OGBench <code>antmaze-{medium,large,giant}-navigate-v0</code>, re-rendered through MuJoCo (<span class="text-tt">osmesa</span>).</li>
	<li>$3$ mazes $\times$ $1{,}000$ episodes $\times \sim 1{,}001$ steps, $224 \times 224 \times 3$ uint8, two cameras (<span class="text-tt">obs_topdown</span>, <span class="text-tt">obs_third</span>).</li>
	<li>Underlying control: $8$-DOF joint torque (OGBench native); <span class="text-tt">qpos</span>: $15$-D, <span class="text-tt">qvel</span>: $14$-D.</li>
	<li>Hindsight relabeling: chunk $K = 16$, terminal-goal fraction $0.25$.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4.2 Task — Coordinate VQA</h2>
<p>
	We frame Ant-Maze navigation as a <b>coordinate VQA</b> on the VLM:
</p>
<ul>
	<li><b>Input:</b> current image $o_t$ (top-view <i>or</i> 3rd-person) plus the instruction <span class="text-tt">"Go to (x, y)."</span></li>
	<li><b>Output:</b> a sequence of <b>intermediate trajectory waypoint coordinates</b> $(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \ldots, (x_K, y_K)$ that the ant should pass through on the way to $(x, y)$.</li>
	<li>A small low-level controller turns those waypoints into the underlying $8$-DOF torques.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4.3 Model — VLM-based VLA, OpenVLA-OFT style</h2>
<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('ant_vla_training.png')}" alt="Ant VLA training pipeline" />
	<span class="caption">
		$(o_t,\;\text{instr},\;\text{proprio}) \rightarrow$ Qwen3-VL 2B
		$\rightarrow$ <b>parallel-decoded waypoint chunk</b>
		$(x_1, y_1), \ldots, (x_K, y_K)$.
	</span>
</div>
<ul>
	<li><b>VLM backbone.</b> <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21631">Qwen3-VL 2B</a>
		(<span class="text-tt">Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct</span>, hidden dim $2048$).</li>
	<li><b>Style.</b> <a href="https://openvla-oft.github.io/">OpenVLA-OFT</a> — both ViT and the LLM head are fine-tuned with LoRA; the action chunk is decoded in parallel.</li>
	<li><b>Output head.</b> Coordinate VQA — the model emits the next $K$ waypoint coordinates as text tokens for $(x_i, y_i)$.</li>
	<li><b>Training data.</b> The AntMaze paired dataset described in 4.1, with the language prompt of 4.2.</li>
</ul>

<div class="block-statement">
	One VLA per camera (top-view vs. 3rd-person). The <b>only difference between the two
	settings is observability</b> — same backbone, same training recipe, same prompts.
</div>

<hr />
<h1>5. Both Views Mean-Collapse — and Top-View is Worse</h1>

<p>
	Naively one might expect the <b>top-view (MDP)</b> agent to be a clean upper
	bound: it sees the underlying state, so the coordinate-VQA task should be
	straightforward. In practice, the top-view trained Qwen3-VL VLA exhibits the
	<b>most severe mean collapse</b> — degenerate gait, twitching in place, falling
	over, getting frozen. Even though the input is the privileged top-view, the
	model produces near-degenerate waypoint sequences, and the low-level controller
	turns those into a degenerate motion.
</p>

<p>
	The <b>3rd-person (POMDP)</b> rollouts also mean-collapse, but the failure
	is <b>less degenerate</b> in motion: the agent actually walks around, explores
	corridors, and occasionally — when the pink goal happens to come into view —
	turns and dives into it. These are <b>opportunistic</b> wins, not principled
	ones.
</p>

<h2>5.1 Mean-collapse failure modes (top-view, MDP)</h2>

<div class="grid-2">
	<div class="figure">
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('fail_falling.mp4')}"></video>
		<span class="caption-muted">
			Top-view, no memory — falling-over collapse. The model emits a near-degenerate
			waypoint sequence, the low-level controller produces a degenerate gait, the
			ant tips over repeatedly.
		</span>
	</div>
	<div class="figure">
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('fail_loop.mp4')}"></video>
		<span class="caption-muted">
			Top-view, no memory — looping/stalled collapse. The agent twitches in place
			and cycles between the same two actions.
		</span>
	</div>
</div>

<h2>5.2 Extreme stuck case — <span class="text-tt">Mean_Collapse_After_40</span></h2>

<div class="figure">
	<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('mean_collapse_after_40.mp4')}"></video>
	<span class="caption-muted">
		Extreme mean collapse on the top-view input. After ~step 40 the predicted
		waypoints collapse to a near-zero mean and the ant is frozen in place for the
		rest of the rollout.
	</span>
</div>

<h2>5.3 Opportunistic 3rd-person rollouts (POMDP)</h2>

<p>
	On the 3rd-person input the same Markovian recipe still mean-collapses, but
	the agent moves more — and sometimes wins by sheer exploration.
</p>

<div class="grid-2">
	<div class="figure">
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('success_00.mp4')}"></video>
		<span class="caption-muted">3rd-person, no memory — opportunistic success.</span>
	</div>
	<div class="figure">
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('success_03.mp4')}"></video>
		<span class="caption-muted">3rd-person, no memory — opportunistic success on a different seed.</span>
	</div>
</div>

<hr />
<h1>6. The Fix — A Memory-Based Steering Module on the Frozen VLA</h1>

<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('steering_module.png')}" alt="Steering module added to the frozen VLA" />
	<span class="caption">
		Learnable steering (peach) builds a fixed-size memory $Z$ from past ViT
		features via a <b>GLA temporal mixer (Linear SSM)</b> + <b>Perceiver
		compression</b>, then injects it into the frozen ViT at L8 / L16 and into the
		action head through gated residuals. The <b>frozen base VLA (green) is never
		re-trained</b>.
	</span>
</div>

<div class="block-statement">
	The steering module is what <b>solves mean collapse</b> on the 3rd-person
	POMDP — recovering the success probability lost by hiding the top-view, and
	surpassing the MDP top-view baseline.
</div>

<h2>6.1 Formulation</h2>

<p>
	Past ViT features $h^{(\ell)}_{t-k}$ at depths $\ell \in \{8, 16\}$ for
	$k = 1, \ldots, K$ are mixed into a fixed-size state by a <b>Gated Linear
	Attention</b> (Linear SSM) with content-gated decay:
</p>

<p>
	$$
	\begin{aligned}
	S_k &= \sigma(g_k) \odot S_{k-1} + K_k^{\top} V_k, \\
	O_k &= Q_k\, S_k.
	\end{aligned}
	$$
</p>

<p>
	The mixed sequence $O^{(\ell)}_{1:K}$ is then compressed to $m = 32$ memory
	tokens by a <b>Perceiver</b> cross-attention with learnable queries
	$Q_{\text{learn}} \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times d}$:
</p>

<p>
	$$
	Z^{(\ell)} \;=\; \mathrm{CrossAttn}\!\left(Q_{\text{learn}},\; O^{(\ell)}_{1:K}\right) \;\in\; \mathbb{R}^{m \times d}.
	$$
</p>

<p>
	The memory is <b>gated-residual injected</b> back into the frozen ViT at
	depths $\ell \in \{8, 16\}$:
</p>

<p>
	$$
	\tilde{h}^{(\ell)}_t \;=\; h^{(\ell)}_t \;+\; \sigma(\alpha_\ell)\,
	\mathrm{CrossAttn}\!\left(h^{(\ell)}_t,\; Z^{(\ell)}\right).
	$$
</p>

<p>And a small residual is added to the frozen action head:</p>

<p>
	$$
	a_t \;=\; \underbrace{\pi_{\text{base}}(o_t)}_{\text{frozen}}
	\;+\; \sigma(\alpha_{\text{res}})\, \Delta\!\left(Z^{(16)}\right).
	$$
</p>

<p>
	Identity-init gates ($\sigma(\alpha) = 0$) make the module a <b>no-op at
	initialization</b>, so it recovers the base VLA exactly and only learns
	corrections.
</p>

<h2>6.2 Why a State-Space Model?</h2>

<ul>
	<li><b>Long sequence.</b> Flattened past is $T = K \cdot N_{\text{patch}} \sim 2{,}000$ tokens. Quadratic attention is $\mathcal{O}(T^2)$; a Linear SSM is $\mathcal{O}(T)$ with a fixed-size state $S$.</li>
	<li><b>Content-gated decay.</b> $\sigma(g_t)$ is a per-token forget — more expressive than RetNet's fixed $\gamma$, more stable than plain linear attention.</li>
	<li><b>Length extrapolation.</b> Train with $K \sim \mathcal{U}[1, 32]$ and sinusoidal temporal PE; remains stable at $K \in \{64, 128\}$ at inference.</li>
	<li><b>Identity-init gates.</b> The module starts as a no-op, recovers the base VLA exactly, then learns corrections safely.</li>
</ul>

<hr />
<h1>7. Bonus — Same Pathology on a GUI Agent</h1>

<p>
	The exact same Markovian-on-$o_t$ failure shows up in a real GUI agent.
	<a href="https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero">GELab-Zero-4B-preview</a>, a
	VLM-based agent, runs on a Duolingo &ldquo;tap the matching pairs&rdquo; quiz.
	At every step it receives the current screenshot and emits Thought + Action.
</p>

<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('gui_agent_case.png')}" alt="Testing GELab-Zero on Duolingo matching quiz" />
	<span class="caption">
		<span class="text-tt">GELab-Zero-4B-preview</span> on a Duolingo matching
		quiz. At every step the agent only sees the current screenshot.
	</span>
</div>

<h2>7.1 The Loop-Case</h2>

<p>
	Solved pairs turn slightly darker, but solved and fresh tiles look almost
	identical — and the agent has no memory of which ones it already tapped. So it
	re-taps a solved pair, the app rejects it, and the screen returns to the same
	state.
</p>

<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('gui_loop_case.png')}" alt="GUI loop case — C → E → C → E …" />
	<span class="caption">
		Loop-case definition. The agent re-taps a solved pair, the app rejects it,
		the screen returns to the same state — $C \rightarrow E \rightarrow C \rightarrow E \rightarrow \ldots$.
		Same pathology as the Ant POMDP: $o_t$ (screenshot) $\not\Rightarrow s_t$
		(solved-set).
	</span>
</div>

<div class="figure">
	<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('gui_rollout.mp4')}"></video>
	<span class="caption-muted">GUI agent rollout (sped up) — getting stuck in the loop.</span>
</div>

<hr />
<h1>8. Takeaway</h1>

<ul>
	<li>On a small Ant-Maze setup, a <b>VLM-based VLA</b> trained on the latest observation only <b>mean-collapses</b> on POMDP inputs — degenerate gait, falling over, frozen agents.</li>
	<li>The task is set up as a <b>coordinate VQA</b> — given <span class="text-tt">Go to (x, y)</span>, the VLM emits intermediate waypoint coordinates. <b>Both views collapse</b>, and the top-view (MDP) collapse is more severe than the 3rd-person (POMDP) one — privileged observation is not enough to escape mean collapse.</li>
	<li>A <b>memory-based steering module on the frozen VLA</b> (GLA Linear-SSM + Perceiver, identity-init gated injection) is the lightweight fix that <b>raises the success rate</b> on the 3rd-person POMDP back above the top-view MDP baseline.</li>
	<li>The Markovian-on-$o_t$ pathology is not specific to robotics — the same loop-case shows up on a GUI agent.</li>
</ul>

<hr />
<h1>References</h1>
<ul>
	<li>Qwen3-VL — <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21631">arxiv.org/abs/2511.21631</a></li>
	<li>OpenVLA-OFT — <a href="https://openvla-oft.github.io/">openvla-oft.github.io</a></li>
	<li>GELab-Zero — <a href="https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero">github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero</a></li>
	<li>OGBench — <a href="https://seohong.me/projects/ogbench/">seohong.me/projects/ogbench</a></li>
	<li>Weasel Toy Experiment (code) — <a href="https://github.com/MK040412/Weasel_toy_experiment">github.com/MK040412/Weasel_toy_experiment</a></li>
</ul>

<h1>Acknowledgement</h1>
<p>
	Sub-project of <a href="https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/">Project Weasel</a>,
	maintained by <a href="https://mk040412.github.io/">Kang Minkyu</a>.
</p>
`
---

<Layout
	title='Mean Collapse is a Serious Bottleneck of VLA'
	pageTitle='Mean Collapse in VLA'
	description='Even on a small Ant-Maze setup, a VLM-based VLA that acts on the latest observation only mean-collapses on POMDP inputs. A small memory-based steering module on the frozen VLA recovers the lost performance.'
	authors={authors}
	links={links}
	hero={fig('ant_hero.png')}
	heroAlt='Ant Maze rollout — 3rd-person view (left) and top-view (right)'
	faviconHref={`${base}/weasel-logo.png`}
>
	<Fragment set:html={body} />
</Layout>
